## Context

§8 Discussion (reading-2 finding 40) introduces three scoring levels and
asserts "The three are perpendicular." But the prose defines **method
quality through table quality**, conflating exactly the two it claims are
orthogonal. The author flags the conflation.

The offending definition (`slides/manuscript/main.tex:1031–1040`):

> \emph{Method quality} asks what the resources buy: the cost-versus-F1
> relation (Figure cost-quality) reads the time and money a method
> spends **against the quality of the table it returns** …

"Method quality" is then literally the relation cost ↔ table-quality —
while "Run quality" is defined as the quality of one table. The two share
the table-quality axis, so calling them perpendicular is internally
inconsistent.

## Relevant files

- `slides/manuscript/main.tex:1031–1046` — the "A result is scored at
  three levels." paragraph.
- Memory/context: the paper's three-quality spine (data / answer /
  method) — method quality is a distinct, perpendicular axis, not a
  function of answer/table quality.

## Actions

1. Rewrite the three-levels paragraph so the axes are genuinely
   independent:
   - **Method quality** = a property of the *method/process* — the
     resources it consumes (time, money) and its reproducibility — to
     reach a result. Independent of how good any single table is.
   - **Run/table quality** = the quality of one output table on the four
     dimensions.
   - **Model quality** = source reliability aggregated over runs.
   The cost-vs-F1 figure *relates* method cost to result quality; it does
   not *define* method quality as table quality. Reword so the figure is
   a relationship between two distinct axes, not a definition of one in
   terms of the other.
2. Keep the "perpendicular" claim only if the rewrite makes it true;
   otherwise drop/soften it.

## Test

- Red first: this is prose-judgment, so the durable guard is a
  **negative** one — e.g. assert the method-quality sentence does not
  define it via "quality of the table it returns" (forbid the specific
  conflating phrasing). Per the CI polarity rule, do not pin the new
  positive wording; record the editorial decision (axes are
  perpendicular, method quality is process-not-output) in
  `docs/editorial-brief.md` instead, checked by /review-pr-prose.

## Verification

- [ ] Method quality is defined as a process/resource property, not as
      cost-vs-table-quality.
- [ ] Run quality and method quality no longer share the table-quality
      axis in their definitions.
- [ ] "Perpendicular" claim is now internally consistent (or removed).
- [ ] Editorial-brief entry added for the decision.
- [ ] `make check` passes; manuscript builds.

## Invariants

- No empirical-number changes. Cost-vs-F1 figure and its data unchanged
  (only the prose framing around it).

## Exit criteria

- The three scoring levels read as genuinely orthogonal; method quality
  is no longer defined through table quality; brief entry recorded;
  `make check` green.
